A||||14||MotherGoose||Gas Tank?||||1||
Z||000000||MotherGoose||06-01-2000||05:04 PM||||My fiero has been sitting for a while and I  think that I should clean the gas tank,( or at least empty and start over). I there anything special I should do?<P>Goose||||reg||1
Z||000001||Linx||06-01-2000||11:08 PM||||Im curious...what makes you think you need to clean your tank?<P>I took mine off (to repair the fuel gauge sender) and it was clean as a whistle.  Never been taken off before.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MotherGoose||06-02-2000||08:43 AM||||Gas has been sitting in it for about a yeaar and a half, and as far as I know there were no additives put in.<P>Goose||||reg||1
Z||000003||lennyb4||06-02-2000||08:48 AM||||I would drain it completely and refill it with high octane and some additive, you shouldnt have any settlement in the tank but adding the octane boost will help clean the entire systyem. Also after you run it for a day or two i would change the fuel filter.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||06-02-2000||09:51 AM||||The Grape Ape sat for over 6 months before I finally got it running.  I haven't touched the gas tank (that's a lie--I put my hand on the bottom of it once) just ran high octane juice through it for the 1st tank full and mid grade through it since.  I haven't even gotten around to replacing the fuel filter yet (next project).  No problems so far.  I really wouldn't worry too much about it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DJRice||06-02-2000||11:56 AM||||Screwing with the gas tank is not something to do unless you have a problem like a dead fuel pump.  Your best bet is to just invest in the fuel system cleaner or octane booster then replace the filter after that stuff gets through the system.<P>My brother ran his Fiero out of Gas 3 times, and we replaced the fuel pump all three times because once you run them dry, they die.  The lifetime warranty is a good thing, but they are a pain in the neck (IMHO) to change.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||MotherGoose||06-02-2000||12:52 PM||||How do you drain the tank without dropping it?  I'm also afraid that the pump is dead.<P>Goose||||reg||1
Z||000007||lennyb4||06-02-2000||01:15 PM||||When i first got my fiero it sat for 1 1/2 years. I took a piece of 3/8" hose about 7 feet long. Hooked on end to a hand powered aquarium pump (or you could do it the good old fashion way) and started the flow. The gas didnt look bad until i got to around the last two gallons (that was really nasty). Afterwards i put in two additional gallons, bounced the car up & down and drained it again. Then i just filled her up with 101 octane (some shell stations carry it) and added boost. Ran it almost dry, changed the filter and walla.... Smooth as a babies bottom..<P>**Sorry for the long post..||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||06-02-2000||01:18 PM||||I'm curious.<P>What does high octane have to do with cleaning out a fuel tank?  A higher octane fuel actually has less BTUs per pound than lower octane fuel.  Higher octane only means that the fuel is harder to ignite than lower octane fuel not that it has more power.<P>I could see a benefit in using a name brand of fuel from a major refiner like Shell or Amoco that has more cleaning additives than an off brand convenience store fuel.<P>The only reason to need increased octane is to prevent pre-detonation due to high compression or a buildup of carbon deposits that glow and ignite the fuel prematurely.<P>Just curious.<P>Have a great day,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000009||tgowens||06-02-2000||01:28 PM||||orrrrrrrrr, you could just add some fuel stabilizer (I use it in my boat every year) if you are really worried about the problem.......I don't think it would be a problem, after all, boats, mowers, power tools all sit through the winter and then start and run easily the next year......and, my girlfriend's '87GT sat in the carport for over a year without even being started - went out one day, turned the key and it fired right up and ran great!! (didn't want to stop well tho as the break pads had just about rusted to the discs!!).......but, that's <I>another</I> story!!<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 06-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||06-02-2000||01:31 PM||||Im with you. I dont know why everyone still thinks Hi Octane, or Premium gas is any better. Your right-it only prevents detonation---nothing else. I know people who drive escorts and fill em with Premium, what a waste of money going down the drain, The name to them just means its got to be much better than regular. My 86 GT has a 2.8 (over to 3.1) and has a aftermarket turbo. It runs just fine and like a bat outta h__l on regular. I put 25K a year on mine-no problems.<P>RG||||reg||1
Z||000011||MotherGoose||06-02-2000||01:55 PM||||I thought that cars starting in 87, had a block in the fill tube that keep you(or others mainly) form syphoning gas out?  True?  False?||||reg||5
Z||000012||MotherGoose||06-02-2000||03:24 PM||||Does anyone know?||||reg||1
Z||000013||fierospeeder||06-02-2000||05:15 PM||||You can go to pepboys or trak auto and buy a siphon with a bulb on it that you can squeeze to pump out the gas.  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Carrolles||06-05-2000||12:07 AM||||Fuel stabilizer needs to be added to gas while it is still fresh.  It slows down the process by which the light ends flash off and leave the varnish buildup on everything.<P>Adding it to old fuel does no good.<BR>||||reg||1
